WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.328 --> 00:00:03.385
(plane taking off)
(bomb bursting)

00:00:03.385 --> 00:00:04.279
- Thicken your skin.

00:00:04.279 --> 00:00:05.263
- Develop a thick skin.

00:00:05.263 --> 00:00:06.555
- You have to have a thick skin.

00:00:06.555 --> 00:00:07.641
- Having thick skin.

00:00:07.641 --> 00:00:08.648
- What's up Believe Nation?

00:00:08.648 --> 00:00:09.481
It's Evan.

00:00:09.481 --> 00:00:10.575
My one word is believe

00:00:10.575 --> 00:00:12.787
and I believe that you have the ability

00:00:12.787 --> 00:00:15.558
to do something special
that will change the planet.

00:00:15.558 --> 00:00:16.818
To help you on your journey,

00:00:16.818 --> 00:00:21.596
today, we're going to learn
how to develop a thicker skin.

00:00:21.596 --> 00:00:25.179
(soaring orchestral music)

00:00:32.582 --> 00:00:34.643
- People always say, "Man,
you cuss all the time, why?"

00:00:34.643 --> 00:00:35.696
(Tom laughs)

00:00:35.696 --> 00:00:37.463
Well, I hate to say it.

00:00:37.463 --> 00:00:40.827
The best way for me to
get how I feel across,

00:00:40.827 --> 00:00:41.660
I can't sit here and say,

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:43.164
"You know what, I went through
a hell of a weekend, man.

00:00:43.164 --> 00:00:44.290
"It was really hard."

00:00:44.290 --> 00:00:45.257
(Tom laughs)

00:00:45.257 --> 00:00:48.951
No, that (bleep) takes your damn soul,

00:00:48.951 --> 00:00:51.665
rips it inside out, and then they say,

00:00:51.665 --> 00:00:56.260
"Now, we're going to (bleep) start."

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:58.807
It allows me to express

00:00:58.807 --> 00:01:02.082
where I was at at a point of my life.

00:01:02.082 --> 00:01:04.164
If I don't give you all of me,

00:01:04.164 --> 00:01:06.246
why the hell am I here?

00:01:06.246 --> 00:01:08.654
How will you learn from me?

00:01:08.654 --> 00:01:11.072
People take so much offense to me.

00:01:11.072 --> 00:01:12.546
You will never learn from people

00:01:12.546 --> 00:01:14.813
if we always tap dance around the truth.

00:01:14.813 --> 00:01:16.235
- Oh, God, I love that.

00:01:16.235 --> 00:01:17.827
- We tap dance around the truth

00:01:17.827 --> 00:01:19.567
by finding the right words

00:01:19.567 --> 00:01:22.217
so I don't hurt you
'cause you have thin skin.

00:01:22.217 --> 00:01:23.550
No,

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:25.181
tighten up people!

00:01:25.181 --> 00:01:26.217
It's okay!

00:01:26.217 --> 00:01:27.801
Trust me, it's okay!

00:01:27.801 --> 00:01:30.066
You might be called nigger one day.

00:01:30.066 --> 00:01:30.899
It's okay.

00:01:31.755 --> 00:01:33.136
You might be called some Jewish word

00:01:33.136 --> 00:01:35.145
or some faggot or gay word.

00:01:35.145 --> 00:01:36.309
It's okay.

00:01:36.309 --> 00:01:37.293
Let 'em call you that.

00:01:37.293 --> 00:01:38.901
What are you going to do now?

00:01:38.901 --> 00:01:40.363
They don't own your life.

00:01:40.363 --> 00:01:42.312
How are you going to control that now?

00:01:42.312 --> 00:01:43.806
How are you going to flip it upside down

00:01:43.806 --> 00:01:45.248
and say,"Roger that."

00:01:45.248 --> 00:01:47.161
Now I'm going to harness this (bleep),

00:01:47.161 --> 00:01:50.441
and you'll read about me years from now.

00:01:50.441 --> 00:01:51.274
How?

00:01:51.274 --> 00:01:52.107
That's the question.

00:01:52.107 --> 00:01:53.805
How are you going to do that?

00:01:53.805 --> 00:01:55.155
Thicken your skin,

00:01:55.155 --> 00:01:56.651
become more of a human being.

00:01:56.651 --> 00:01:57.982
Don't be afraid

00:01:57.982 --> 00:01:59.682
of the reflection in the mirror

00:01:59.682 --> 00:02:02.231
'cause that's all you can be afraid of.

00:02:02.231 --> 00:02:06.017
Once you overcome the
reflection in the mirror,

00:02:06.017 --> 00:02:06.850
you've done it.

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:09.306
- The best defense to

00:02:09.306 --> 00:02:12.069
speech that you don't like about yourself

00:02:12.069 --> 00:02:15.638
as a public figure is
to develop a thick skin.

00:02:15.638 --> 00:02:18.798
It's really the only effective defense

00:02:18.798 --> 00:02:21.054
because you can't stop it.

00:02:21.054 --> 00:02:22.801
You are going to be misunderstood.

00:02:22.801 --> 00:02:25.364
If you're doing anything
interesting in the world,

00:02:25.364 --> 00:02:27.708
you're going to have critics.

00:02:27.708 --> 00:02:29.978
If you absolutely can't tolerate critics,

00:02:29.978 --> 00:02:32.477
then don't do anything new or interesting.

00:02:32.477 --> 00:02:34.337
(audience laughs)

00:02:34.337 --> 00:02:35.914
Then you can insulate yourself,

00:02:35.914 --> 00:02:37.813
then think how wonderful
your life will be.

00:02:37.813 --> 00:02:38.953
- Is that the Bezos' principle?

00:02:38.953 --> 00:02:39.786
- Yeah.

00:02:39.786 --> 00:02:41.409
- Before I leave your early life,

00:02:41.409 --> 00:02:44.329
I also heard that you went to a school

00:02:44.329 --> 00:02:47.692
that was out of your
initial neighborhood--

00:02:47.692 --> 00:02:49.912
- Yeah, I was bused to school.

00:02:49.912 --> 00:02:51.245
It was pretty...

00:02:53.527 --> 00:02:54.360
Whatever, man.

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:58.347
People were mean and they
called you nigger and whatever,

00:02:58.347 --> 00:03:02.027
and spit on you, and
threw feces, and piss,

00:03:02.027 --> 00:03:03.694
and whatever at you.

00:03:05.504 --> 00:03:06.461
It is what it is.

00:03:06.461 --> 00:03:07.659
It's nothing I can do about it.

00:03:07.659 --> 00:03:09.326
It made me stronger.

00:03:11.579 --> 00:03:13.579
I'm not here without it.

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:19.207
You can't phase me with
meanness because of it.

00:03:22.513 --> 00:03:23.430
That was my

00:03:24.728 --> 00:03:26.426
grade school experience,

00:03:26.426 --> 00:03:28.397
grade school, high school,
junior high school.

00:03:28.397 --> 00:03:31.349
- Who is an entrepreneur where
everything is a smooth ride?

00:03:31.349 --> 00:03:32.764
You go through these
things and I'll tell you,

00:03:32.764 --> 00:03:35.203
and you get criticized, and
you have to have a thick skin.

00:03:35.203 --> 00:03:36.036
Yeah.

00:03:37.114 --> 00:03:38.092
- [Interviewer] You've been there?

00:03:38.092 --> 00:03:38.925
- I've been there.

00:03:38.925 --> 00:03:41.159
(audience laughs)
By the way,

00:03:41.159 --> 00:03:42.555
you don't buy a thick skin.

00:03:42.555 --> 00:03:43.783
You don't even know you have it

00:03:43.783 --> 00:03:46.369
until somebody starts
to try to puncture it.

00:03:46.369 --> 00:03:48.128
Then you discover what you're made of.

00:03:48.128 --> 00:03:49.913
- One of the greatest,
and probably the greatest,

00:03:49.913 --> 00:03:51.224
rock climbers of this generation,

00:03:51.224 --> 00:03:52.738
as you know I've been a
climber most of my life,

00:03:52.738 --> 00:03:55.759
is actually right now on
the side of El Capitan

00:03:55.759 --> 00:03:58.927
doing what will probably
be the hardest rock climb

00:03:58.927 --> 00:04:01.308
certainly ever done in our lifetimes,

00:04:01.308 --> 00:04:03.263
trying to free climb a
thing called the Dawn Wall,

00:04:03.263 --> 00:04:05.316
on the Mescalito side of El Cap.

00:04:05.316 --> 00:04:07.864
A young man named Tommy Caldwell who is...

00:04:07.864 --> 00:04:09.865
He's in the category of one.

00:04:09.865 --> 00:04:11.655
Now I was out climbing with Tommy one day

00:04:11.655 --> 00:04:13.707
and I said to him,

00:04:13.707 --> 00:04:15.075
"Tommy, what makes you different?"

00:04:15.075 --> 00:04:16.834
He has six climbs on El Cap

00:04:16.834 --> 00:04:19.893
that have never been repeated.

00:04:19.893 --> 00:04:23.270
He is 10X in extreme environments

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:25.069
and he's still alive.

00:04:25.069 --> 00:04:26.633
I said, "Tommy, what is it that you have?

00:04:26.633 --> 00:04:28.795
"You're not necessarily
more physically gifted.

00:04:28.795 --> 00:04:30.613
"You're not necessarily stronger.

00:04:30.613 --> 00:04:31.905
"All right, you're
certainly physically gifted

00:04:31.905 --> 00:04:32.738
"and all those things,

00:04:32.738 --> 00:04:35.288
"but you're not this incredible athlete

00:04:35.288 --> 00:04:37.901
"that just was born this way."

00:04:37.901 --> 00:04:39.738
In fact, he had lost a
finger in an accident.

00:04:39.738 --> 00:04:41.090
He cut a finger off.

00:04:41.090 --> 00:04:43.671
So he's doing this minus one of his--

00:04:43.671 --> 00:04:44.576
- [Interviewer] An important finger.

00:04:44.576 --> 00:04:46.698
- An important finger.

00:04:46.698 --> 00:04:48.306
He thought about it for a long moment

00:04:48.306 --> 00:04:49.306
and he said,

00:04:50.317 --> 00:04:53.567
"I can remain focused on and suffer for

00:04:55.019 --> 00:04:58.352
"the big thing longer than anyone else."

00:05:02.091 --> 00:05:02.981
The curiosity keeps him going,

00:05:02.981 --> 00:05:04.952
but once they get their hands on

00:05:04.952 --> 00:05:06.921
what they see as the thing,

00:05:06.921 --> 00:05:08.992
they don't let go!

00:05:08.992 --> 00:05:11.452
They can stay with it and suffer for it.

00:05:11.452 --> 00:05:13.861
- I've been doing this
long enough to realize

00:05:13.861 --> 00:05:17.123
that no matter what people perceive of me,

00:05:17.123 --> 00:05:21.155
I always shine through
at the end of the day.

00:05:21.155 --> 00:05:23.891
I've had a lot of people
try to tear me down,

00:05:23.891 --> 00:05:25.181
but I'm here,

00:05:25.181 --> 00:05:27.032
and I've always been myself,

00:05:27.032 --> 00:05:28.672
and I've always wanted that.

00:05:28.672 --> 00:05:30.332
I've been graceful

00:05:30.332 --> 00:05:32.465
and I've tried to handle
myself with as much class.

00:05:32.465 --> 00:05:35.664
It's only because I know how hard I work,

00:05:35.664 --> 00:05:37.606
and I'm not going to let
any of that get to me.

00:05:37.606 --> 00:05:40.308
- First bit of advice
came from Tyler Perry,

00:05:40.308 --> 00:05:43.465
the famous director,
kind of movie producer,

00:05:43.465 --> 00:05:45.042
that sort of thing, right?

00:05:45.042 --> 00:05:47.674
About two years ago,
I had the good fortune

00:05:47.674 --> 00:05:49.523
to interview him one-on-one
in three different cities

00:05:49.523 --> 00:05:51.636
around the country where he got to speak

00:05:51.636 --> 00:05:53.778
about his entrepreneurial
story to a bunch of

00:05:53.778 --> 00:05:55.354
small business owners around the country.

00:05:55.354 --> 00:05:57.792
Now, Tyler Perry is the key...

00:05:57.792 --> 00:05:58.625
Did his thing.

00:05:58.625 --> 00:06:00.088
He was homeless for awhile,

00:06:00.088 --> 00:06:03.319
now he's like the highest
paid dude in Hollywood,

00:06:03.319 --> 00:06:05.371
a really fascinating story.

00:06:05.371 --> 00:06:07.728
So I'm interviewing him one-on-one,

00:06:07.728 --> 00:06:08.683
start the Q and A,

00:06:08.683 --> 00:06:10.633
one woman raises her hand,

00:06:10.633 --> 00:06:13.762
stands up, she says, "Mr. Perry,

00:06:13.762 --> 00:06:15.347
"we have to always go through
these different trials

00:06:15.347 --> 00:06:16.908
"and tribulations as entrepreneurs.

00:06:16.908 --> 00:06:19.236
"What keeps you going?

00:06:19.236 --> 00:06:22.020
"How do you get back up and just go?"

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:24.080
He said the most profound
thing that I had ever heard.

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:26.937
He said the minute he realized

00:06:26.937 --> 00:06:29.854
that the trials that you go through

00:06:31.546 --> 00:06:35.500
and the blessings you receive
are the exact same thing,

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:38.071
that freed him as an entrepreneur.

00:06:38.071 --> 00:06:40.346
Just think about that for a second.

00:06:40.346 --> 00:06:41.983
I kind of stopped the interview

00:06:41.983 --> 00:06:44.482
because I had to soak
that up for a little bit.

00:06:44.482 --> 00:06:46.636
Like every single kind
of trial or tribulation

00:06:46.636 --> 00:06:47.794
and all that stuff that you go through,

00:06:47.794 --> 00:06:49.254
it's just a lesson.

00:06:49.254 --> 00:06:52.141
That lesson is just an inherent blessing.

00:06:52.141 --> 00:06:53.596
As entrepreneurs,

00:06:53.596 --> 00:06:55.745
this entrepreneur thing is hard.

00:06:55.745 --> 00:06:57.310
At times it's amazing,

00:06:57.310 --> 00:07:00.339
at times it sucks (chuckles).

00:07:00.339 --> 00:07:01.862
I have been very blessed and fortunate

00:07:01.862 --> 00:07:03.349
to have really good opportunities,

00:07:03.349 --> 00:07:05.911
but it's very, very hard.

00:07:05.911 --> 00:07:08.035
You don't want to stress
yourself out unnecessarily.

00:07:08.035 --> 00:07:10.016
What that lesson taught me

00:07:10.016 --> 00:07:12.677
was that I didn't have
to be as stressed out.

00:07:12.677 --> 00:07:14.265
I'm going to go through these issues,

00:07:14.265 --> 00:07:15.409
they're just lessons,

00:07:15.409 --> 00:07:17.096
let me treat that as a blessing,

00:07:17.096 --> 00:07:18.520
we're going to move.

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:20.177
A lot of folks looked at me
when I started this company

00:07:20.177 --> 00:07:22.532
like, "Tristan, you're way
more calm than you should be."

00:07:22.532 --> 00:07:25.051
And it's like, "Yeah, because
I fundamentally understand

00:07:25.051 --> 00:07:26.475
"this lesson that I learned."

00:07:26.475 --> 00:07:28.628
- Success has nothing
to do with potential.

00:07:28.628 --> 00:07:30.733
It's all about the
perseverance of somebody.

00:07:30.733 --> 00:07:32.724
That'll override any endeavor.

00:07:32.724 --> 00:07:34.024
If you throw (bleep) against the wall,

00:07:34.024 --> 00:07:35.632
eventually something will stick.

00:07:35.632 --> 00:07:37.133
You guys that are "I have this potential"

00:07:37.133 --> 00:07:38.850
or "I don't have this potential,"

00:07:38.850 --> 00:07:40.408
that (bleep) dies.

00:07:40.408 --> 00:07:42.228
What continues to last forever

00:07:42.228 --> 00:07:44.280
is the perseverance to always show up.

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:45.348
That's what champions do.

00:07:45.348 --> 00:07:48.182
Every single champion is the
same as every ordinary person.

00:07:48.182 --> 00:07:49.770
The only differential is that they show up

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:51.354
to the event every single day.

00:07:51.354 --> 00:07:53.265
They see failure as a learning curve.

00:07:53.265 --> 00:07:54.769
They welcome failure.

00:07:54.769 --> 00:07:55.825
You learn more from failure

00:07:55.825 --> 00:07:58.234
than you ever will from success.

00:07:58.234 --> 00:07:59.980
So showing up and getting
knocked on your ass,

00:07:59.980 --> 00:08:01.597
find the intestinal
fortitude, stand back up,

00:08:01.597 --> 00:08:03.588
and reface that endeavor.

00:08:03.588 --> 00:08:05.424
It's going to be the overriding factor

00:08:05.424 --> 00:08:06.777
that makes a difference.

00:08:06.777 --> 00:08:08.151
How do you know when
you're doing those things?

00:08:08.151 --> 00:08:09.698
The small things in life add up

00:08:09.698 --> 00:08:11.333
to those big monumental things.

00:08:11.333 --> 00:08:12.184
Once you do small things,

00:08:12.184 --> 00:08:13.628
it's a character building block

00:08:13.628 --> 00:08:15.094
that your name means something.

00:08:15.094 --> 00:08:16.598
You hold value to your name.

00:08:16.598 --> 00:08:18.404
If you drop trash on the
ground, you pick it up

00:08:18.404 --> 00:08:19.400
because that's your responsibility.

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:20.925
You hold value to yourself.

00:08:20.925 --> 00:08:21.758
When you shake someone's hand,

00:08:21.758 --> 00:08:23.646
you look them in the eye,
give them a firm handshake

00:08:23.646 --> 00:08:25.727
'cause you're here for a purpose.

00:08:25.727 --> 00:08:27.650
Everything you do, you do
to the best of your ability.

00:08:27.650 --> 00:08:29.183
It doesn't matter what you're doing,

00:08:29.183 --> 00:08:30.564
it's the fact that you're doing it

00:08:30.564 --> 00:08:32.352
so therefore it means something to you.

00:08:32.352 --> 00:08:33.978
That's going to build a legacy

00:08:33.978 --> 00:08:36.306
'cause the title out there
doesn't mean (bleep).

00:08:36.306 --> 00:08:38.094
You win the title, fine.

00:08:38.094 --> 00:08:40.114
That title will not get you another title.

00:08:40.114 --> 00:08:42.096
What you're trying to build is legacy,

00:08:42.096 --> 00:08:44.979
footprints behind you that
are left as motivation

00:08:44.979 --> 00:08:46.200
for someone else to follow.

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:48.810
Legacy is built daily
through the character,

00:08:48.810 --> 00:08:52.548
and the willpower, the code
of conduct within somebody.

00:08:52.548 --> 00:08:53.604
That's the perseverance,

00:08:53.604 --> 00:08:55.424
showing up every single day.

00:08:55.424 --> 00:08:57.119
That will always achieve your goal.

00:08:57.119 --> 00:08:58.643
It doesn't matter what you're born with,

00:08:58.643 --> 00:08:59.841
it doesn't matter your potential,

00:08:59.841 --> 00:09:02.250
how much silver spoon or money you have,

00:09:02.250 --> 00:09:04.759
all that can be attained if you show up

00:09:04.759 --> 00:09:09.310
and you're willing to
risk failure for success.

00:09:09.310 --> 00:09:10.276
That's it.

00:09:10.276 --> 00:09:11.829
- It could also remind us of success

00:09:11.829 --> 00:09:14.307
of Alexander Bell, the
inventor of telephone,

00:09:14.307 --> 00:09:15.890
who had a stack of notebooks

00:09:15.890 --> 00:09:19.504
where he recorded every failed experiment.

00:09:19.504 --> 00:09:22.127
So if you work really hard,

00:09:22.127 --> 00:09:23.127
you can find

00:09:24.170 --> 00:09:28.149
the winning spot even in the midst of your

00:09:28.149 --> 00:09:29.649
numerous failures.

00:09:31.173 --> 00:09:34.107
I think the ex-Speaker of
US House of Representatives,

00:09:34.107 --> 00:09:35.274
Newt Gingrich,

00:09:36.962 --> 00:09:39.173
hit the right points when he said that,

00:09:39.173 --> 00:09:41.673
"Perseverance is the hard work

00:09:42.540 --> 00:09:45.240
"you do after you get
tired of doing hard work

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:46.360
"you already did."

00:09:46.360 --> 00:09:47.610
- When I was 18

00:09:48.510 --> 00:09:51.485
or 17, I graduated high school,

00:09:51.485 --> 00:09:54.994
and I moved out and became
a door-to-door salesman.

00:09:54.994 --> 00:09:56.761
- [Interviewer] Selling
Kirby vacuum cleaners?

00:09:56.761 --> 00:09:59.344
- No, selling much lower priced

00:10:00.331 --> 00:10:05.097
items, coupons for businesses
in the service industry

00:10:05.097 --> 00:10:07.271
for oil changes for $20,

00:10:07.271 --> 00:10:08.112
three or four trips

00:10:08.112 --> 00:10:09.924
to a day spa for $35.
- You were selling a packet?

00:10:09.924 --> 00:10:11.274
- The packet?
- Yeah, it was just...

00:10:11.274 --> 00:10:12.592
- No, just one coupon.

00:10:12.592 --> 00:10:13.425
I'd walk up and be like,

00:10:13.425 --> 00:10:14.517
"Hi, I'm from Meineke.

00:10:14.517 --> 00:10:16.750
"Here you go, four oil changes, $20.

00:10:16.750 --> 00:10:18.029
"Pretty great, right?

